   "id": "B",
   "skill": "R",
   "title": "Reading（短め）：選択＋TRUE/FALSE/NOT GIVEN",
   "limitMin": 10,
   "instructions": "Questions 1–3: Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.\nQuestions 4–6: Do the following statements agree with the information given in the reading passage? Write TRUE if the statement agrees with the information, FALSE if the statement contradicts the information, NOT GIVEN if there is no information on this.",
   "directions": "選択問題は先に設問のキーワード（年代・固有名詞）に印をつけ、本文で該当箇所を探してから選択肢を読む。",
   "passage": "# The Return of the Night Train\n\nFor much of the twentieth century, overnight trains were a common way to travel long distances across Europe. Passengers could board in the evening, sleep in a small cabin and wake up in another country the following morning. From the 1990s onwards, however, many of these services were cancelled. Low-cost airlines offered flights that were faster and often less expensive, and railway companies found that the old sleeping carriages were costly to maintain. By the early 2010s, only a handful of international night trains were still running, and many people assumed that this form of travel would soon disappear completely.\n\nIn recent years, this trend has started to reverse. A major reason is growing concern about climate change. A train journey produces far less carbon dioxide per passenger than a flight over the same distance, and a number of travellers, particularly younger ones, now say they are willing to spend more time travelling in order to reduce their impact on the environment. Some governments have also begun to support rail travel, for example by reducing taxes on train tickets or by providing money for new routes.\n\nNight trains offer practical advantages too. Because passengers sleep during the journey, they do not lose a working day, and they arrive in the city centre rather than at an airport far from town. For business travellers, this can make an overnight train more convenient than an early morning flight, which may require leaving home before dawn. Families with young children also tend to appreciate the extra space, since children can move around more freely than on a plane.\n\nNevertheless, the revival faces serious obstacles. Modern sleeping carriages are expensive to build, and because each cabin holds only a few passengers, a night train carries far fewer people than a daytime train of the same length. As a result, the price of a private cabin can be higher than the cost of a flight and a hotel room combined. Operators must also deal with different rules in each country, including different safety standards and electrical systems, which can cause delays at borders.\n\nThe operator EuroLuna offers one example of how these problems might be addressed. Instead of offering only private cabins, its trains include a range of options, from simple reclining seats to small sleeping pods for a single traveller. The company says that the pods, which are cheaper than cabins but more private than seats, have been especially popular with students. It has also signed agreements with several national railways so that its trains can cross borders without long stops. Early figures suggest that more than 70 per cent of its seats and pods are booked on a typical night.\n\nWhether night trains can become a mainstream form of transport again is still unclear. They are unlikely to replace flights on very long routes, where a journey would take more than one night. On routes of between 600 and 1,500 kilometres, however, many experts believe they have a real chance of attracting passengers back from the air.",
   "passageJa": "夜行列車の復活\n\n20世紀の大半の間、夜行列車はヨーロッパを長距離移動する一般的な手段だった。乗客は夕方に乗車し、小さな個室で眠り、翌朝には別の国で目を覚ますことができた。しかし1990年代以降、こうした路線の多くが廃止された。格安航空会社がより速く、しばしばより安い便を提供し、鉄道会社は古い寝台車の維持に費用がかかることに気づいた。2010年代初めには、まだ運行している国際夜行列車はほんの一握りで、この旅の形はまもなく完全に消えるだろうと多くの人が考えていた。\n\n近年、この傾向は逆転し始めている。大きな理由は気候変動への関心の高まりだ。列車での移動は同じ距離の飛行機に比べて乗客一人あたりの二酸化炭素の排出がはるかに少なく、多くの旅行者、特に若い人たちは、環境への影響を減らすためなら移動により多くの時間をかけてもよいと今では言っている。列車の切符にかかる税金を下げたり、新しい路線に資金を出したりして、鉄道の旅を支援し始めた政府もある。\n\n夜行列車には実用的な利点もある。乗客は移動中に眠るので一日の仕事時間を失わず、町から遠い空港ではなく市の中心部に到着する。出張者にとって、これは夜明け前に家を出る必要があるかもしれない早朝の便より夜行列車を便利にしうる。幼い子どものいる家族も、子どもが飛行機の中より自由に動き回れるので、広い空間をありがたく思う傾向がある。\n\nそれでも、この復活は深刻な障害に直面している。現代の寝台車は製造費が高く、各個室には数人しか乗れないため、夜行列車は同じ長さの昼間の列車よりはるかに少ない人数しか運べない。その結果、個室の値段が飛行機代とホテル代の合計より高くなることもある。運行会社は、安全基準や電気方式の違いなど、国ごとに異なる規則にも対応しなければならず、それが国境での遅れを引き起こすことがある。\n\n運行会社ユーロルナは、これらの問題にどう対処しうるかを示す一つの例である。個室だけを提供するのではなく、同社の列車には簡単なリクライニングシートから一人用の小さな寝台ポッドまで、さまざまな選択肢がある。同社によれば、個室より安く座席よりプライバシーがあるポッドは、特に学生に人気だという。また、自社の列車が国境で長く停車せずに通過できるよう、いくつかの国の鉄道会社と協定を結んでいる。初期の数字では、通常の夜には座席とポッドの70パーセント以上が予約で埋まっているという。\n\n夜行列車が再び主流の交通手段になれるかどうかは、まだはっきりしない。移動に一晩以上かかるような非常に長い路線で飛行機に取って代わる可能性は低い。しかし600〜1,500キロメートルの路線では、乗客を空の旅から呼び戻す現実的な見込みがあると多くの専門家は考えている。",
